1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,218
This is one teaspoon.

2
00:00:02,218 --> 00:00:09,401
Five millilitres. Fill it with matter from inside
a neutron star, and it would outweigh a mountain.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:11,150
Billions of tonnes.

4
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:17,702
More than every human being on Earth, combined.
So what would it do, if you brought it home?

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,728
The honest answer is
stranger than the question.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,045
It begins with a giant star, at least
eight times the mass of our Sun.

7
00:00:32,045 --> 00:00:39,320
For millions of years it shines. Then its core
fills with iron, and iron gives no energy back.

8
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:41,549
The fire goes out.

9
00:00:41,549 --> 00:00:55,168
In less than a second, a core thousands of kilometres wide falls in on itself, to a ball
about twenty kilometres across. The rest of the star is blown into space. A supernova.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,793
What is left behind is a neutron star.

11
00:01:00,793 --> 00:01:09,156
Here is one, to scale, above New York. About twenty-five
kilometres across. A little longer than Manhattan.

12
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:14,329
And packed inside that sphere is
more mass than the entire Sun.

13
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:19,491
Ordinary atoms are almost
entirely empty space.

14
00:01:19,491 --> 00:01:32,164
Here, that space has been crushed out. Electrons and protons are forced together
into neutrons, packed about as tightly as the inside of an atomic nucleus.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,599
That is where the teaspoon comes from.

16
00:01:35,599 --> 00:01:48,476
Near the surface, the crust is far less dense. But averaged through the whole star, five
millilitres holds about two billion tonnes. Deep in the core, perhaps five billion, or more.

17
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,407
Gravity at the surface is around a hundred
billion times stronger than on Earth.

18
00:01:55,407 --> 00:02:04,288
Drop something from one metre, and it lands in about a millionth
of a second, at well over a thousand kilometres per second.

19
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,236
To leave again, you would need
more than half the speed of light.

20
00:02:09,236 --> 00:02:15,927
Gravity here is strong enough to bend light
itself, so you can see part of the star's far side.

21
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:18,430
And they spin.

22
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:26,952
Beams of radiation sweep out from the magnetic poles,
and if one crosses the Earth, we see a pulse. A pulsar.

23
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:32,634
The fastest one known turns seven
hundred and sixteen times every second.

24
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:37,189
Its equator moves at nearly a
quarter of the speed of light.

25
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:45,530
Some, called magnetars, carry magnetic fields around
a thousand trillion times stronger than the Earth's.

26
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:47,985
The strongest magnets known.

27
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,550
So. The teaspoon. Here is the problem:

28
00:02:52,550 --> 00:03:00,767
you could not scoop it. This matter is only that dense
because the weight of an entire star is pressing down on it.

29
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:06,592
Take a spoonful away from that
gravity, and nothing holds it together.

30
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:17,148
It would expand, instantly, and violently. By rough estimates, the blast
would dwarf the asteroid impact that ended the age of the dinosaurs.

31
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:24,585
But suppose, purely as a thought experiment,
that it somehow stayed compressed.

32
00:03:24,585 --> 00:03:29,380
A bead about two centimetres
wide, weighing billions of tonnes.

33
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:36,217
Within a few metres of it, its gravity would
pull on you harder than the whole Earth does.

34
00:03:36,217 --> 00:03:40,251
And no spoon, no floor, no
bedrock could hold it up.

35
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,091
It would fall straight through the ground,
and the planet would barely slow it.

36
00:03:46,091 --> 00:03:55,034
Down to the centre in about twenty minutes. On, almost to the far
side. And back. Again, and again, through the heart of the world.

37
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,427
Sometimes, though, neutron stars
really do give their matter back.

38
00:04:01,427 --> 00:04:08,097
When two of them orbit each other, they spiral
together over millions of years, and collide.

39
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:16,034
On the seventeenth of August, 2017, detectors on
Earth felt the ripples in space from exactly that:

40
00:04:16,034 --> 00:04:24,533
a merger, a hundred and thirty million light-years away.
Then telescopes watched the wreckage glow. A kilonova.

41
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:33,235
This time, the matter did escape. And as it expanded,
it forged heavy elements. Gold and platinum among them:

42
00:04:33,235 --> 00:04:38,695
by some estimates, several times
the mass of the Earth in gold alone.

43
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:43,904
So the gold in a wedding ring
was very likely made like this.

44
00:04:43,904 --> 00:04:46,592
In the collision of dead stars.

45
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:52,160
A neutron star is the densest
matter we can observe.

46
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:58,343
Add a little more mass, and nothing can
hold it up at all. It becomes a black hole.

47
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:03,170
So you were never going to hold
a teaspoon of a neutron star.

48
00:05:03,170 --> 00:05:06,574
But you may already be
wearing a little of one.

